  pEn=[
   "For a thousand years and more, the Welsh knew where they came from. They were the Britons, the first people of the Island of Britain, and their first king was Brutus, a prince of the royal house of Troy. The monks wrote the story in their chronicles, the poets sang it, the princes of Wales wrote it in their letters, and a Welsh king carried it to the throne of England. Welsh tradition holds that the blood of Troy runs in the veins of the Cymry still. This is the story, and how it shaped Wales.",
   "|| Who | Brutus, Brut in Welsh, great-grandson of Aeneas of Troy",
   "|| His wife | Ignoge, daughter of Pandrasus, king of the Greeks",
   "|| Landed | At Totnes, on the coast of Devon",
   "|| When | In the days of Eli the priest, in the reckoning of the old chronicles: about 1100 BC",
   "|| His city | New Troy on the Thames, later Caer Lud, London",
   "|| His sons | Locrinus, Camber and Albanactus",
   "|| Camber’s share | Cambria: all the land beyond the Severn",
   "|| First written down | The History of the Britons, Gwynedd, about 829",
   "|| Told in full | Geoffrey of Monmouth, about 1136, and the Welsh Brut y Brenhinedd",
   "## The prince of Troy",
   "When Troy fell to the Greeks, the hero Aeneas escaped from the burning city with his son Ascanius and sailed west to Italy, where his line would one day found Rome. The Welsh story begins two generations later. Ascanius had a son, Silvius, and when the wife of Silvius was with child, the wise men foretold that the boy would be the death of his father and his mother, and that after wandering in exile through many lands he would rise to the highest honour. His mother died when he was born. They named him Brutus.",
   "When Brutus was fifteen he went hunting with his father, and an arrow he loosed at a stag struck Silvius by mischance and killed him. Banished from Italy for the deed, the young prince went east, into Greece.",
   "## The Trojans set free",
   "In Greece he found his own people. The descendants of Helenus, son of King Priam of Troy, were held in bondage there by Pandrasus, king of the Greeks. When they learned that a prince of the blood of Troy had come among them, they chose him for their leader. With the help of Assaracus, a young Greek noble whose mother was a Trojan, Brutus led them into the woods and hills, and he beat the Greeks in battle after battle until Pandrasus himself was taken prisoner. For his freedom the king gave the Trojans ships, gold and silver, corn and wine for a long voyage, and his eldest daughter, Ignoge, to be Brutus’s wife.",
   "## The island in the west",
   "Two days and a night out to sea, the fleet came to a deserted island called Leogecia. In a ruined city there they found a temple of the goddess Diana. Brutus made his offerings before her image and lay down to sleep before the altar, and in a dream the goddess stood beside him and spoke:",
   "> Brute, past the realms of Gaul, beneath the sunset, lieth an Island, girt about by ocean, guarded by ocean, erst the haunt of giants, desert of late, and meet for this thy people. Seek it! For there is thine abode for ever. There by thy sons again shall Troy be builded; there of thy blood shall Kings be born, hereafter sovran in every land the wide world over. | Diana to Brutus, in Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History, translated by Sebastian Evans (1904)",
   "Brutus woke and told his companions, and they set sail with joy for the island in the west. On the way they met another company of Trojan exiles by the Tyrrhene Sea, led by Corineus, a man of giant strength and great heart, and he and his people joined them. In Gaul the Trojans fought the people of Poitou and won, though Brutus lost his nephew Turonus in the fighting. Turonus was buried there, and the city of Tours, the old books say, bears his name.",
   "## Landing at Totnes",
   "At last the fleet came to the promised island, and Brutus came ashore at Totnes, on the coast of Devon. The island was called Albion then, and no one lived there but a few giants. It was a fair land, with rivers full of fish and woods full of game, and Brutus and his people loved it at once. He named the island Britain after himself, so that his memory should live on in its name, and his companions were called Britons. Their language, which had been called Trojan, was called British from that day. Welsh tradition holds that this British tongue is the language the Cymry speak today.",
   "In Totnes they still keep the Brutus Stone, set in the pavement of Fore Street, where tradition says he first stepped ashore and said: Here I stand and here I rest.",
   "Corineus was given the country at the western tip of the island and called it after himself: Cornwall. There he wrestled with the greatest of the giants, Goemagot, twelve cubits tall. The giant broke three of his ribs, but Corineus heaved him up onto his shoulders and threw him from a cliff into the sea. The place was called Goemagot’s Leap ever after.",
   "## New Troy",
   "Brutus looked for a place for his chief city, and found it on the river Thames. He called it Troia Nova, New Troy. Over the years the name was worn down to Trinovantum, until King Lud, many generations later, rebuilt its walls and towers and it was called Caer Lud, Lud’s city, the name from which, Geoffrey says, London comes. The old chronicles set the founding of New Troy in the days when Eli the priest ruled in Judaea and the Ark of the Covenant was taken by the Philistines.",
   "## Three sons, three lands",
   "Brutus gave his people laws and ruled them in peace. He died in the twenty-fourth year after he landed, and his sons buried him in New Troy. He had three sons by Ignoge, and they shared the island between them. Locrinus, the eldest, took the middle of the island, which was called Loegria after him: Lloegr, the Welsh name for England to this day. Albanactus, the youngest, took the north, which was called Albany after him: Yr Alban, Scotland. And Camber took all the land beyond the river Severn.",
   "> Which afterward was for a long time called Cambria, after his name. | Geoffrey of Monmouth on the land of Camber, translated by Sebastian Evans (1904)",
   "Welsh tradition holds that this is the name of Wales herself: Cambria, the land of Camber. Scholars today trace the word Cymry, the name the Welsh call themselves, to an old British word, combrogi, meaning fellow countrymen, the people of one land. Either way the name says the same thing: that the Welsh belong together, and belong here.",
   "The rivers kept the story too. When Albanactus was killed by Humber, a king from over the sea, his brothers drove the invader into a great river, where he drowned and left his name to it: the Humber. And the Severn, Hafren in Welsh, takes its name, the story says, from Habren, a princess of the line of Brutus, who was lost in its waters.",
   "## Where the story was written down",
   "The story first appears in writing in the History of the Britons, the Historia Brittonum, gathered in Gwynedd about 829, in the reign of Merfyn Frych. It says plainly that the Island of Britain takes its name from Brutus. It tells how he was born of the line of Aeneas, killed his father by mischance, was driven out of Italy, founded the city of Tours, and came at last to this island, which he filled with his people. It dates his reign to the days of Eli the priest. The writer also gives a second account, from ‘the ancient books of our ancestors’, which traces Brutus back through the first families of Europe to Japheth, son of Noah.",
   "Then, about 1136, Geoffrey of Monmouth, a man of Gwent, wrote the History of the Kings of Britain, the Historia Regum Britanniae. He said that he had turned into Latin ‘a very old book in the British tongue’, brought out of Wales by Walter, archdeacon of Oxford. It told the whole story of the Britons, from Brutus to Cadwaladr, the last king of the Britons to wear the crown of the island: Leir and his daughters, Belinus and Brennius who took Rome, Lud who built the walls of London, Caswallon who stood against Julius Caesar, Macsen, Gwrtheyrn, Emrys and Arthur. It was one of the most read books of the Middle Ages, and more than two hundred manuscripts of it survive.",
   "In Geoffrey’s story, when Julius Caesar first looked across the sea at Britain, he said:",
   "> We Romans and these Britons be of one ancestry, for we also do come of Trojan stock. | Julius Caesar, in Geoffrey of Monmouth’s History, translated by Sebastian Evans (1904)",
   "And Caswallon, king of the Britons, wrote back to him that the same noble blood of Aeneas beat in the hearts of Briton and Roman alike, and that the Britons would fight for their freedom as their fathers had. The Britons and the Romans, in the Welsh story, were cousins: two branches of the royal house of Troy.",
   "## Brut y Brenhinedd",
   "In Wales, Geoffrey’s book came home. Within a hundred years it had been turned into Welsh, not once but several times over, as Brut y Brenhinedd, the Chronicle of the Kings. Some sixty copies survive, the oldest from the middle of the thirteenth century. No book was copied more often in medieval Wales.",
   "So well loved was the story that the name of Brutus became a Welsh word. A brut, in Welsh, is a chronicle, a history, and that is why the chronicle of the princes is called Brut y Tywysogion. In their great manuscripts, such as the Red Book of Hergest, the Welsh set their history down as one long story. First came Ystorya Dared, the Welsh telling of the fall of Troy. Then came Brut y Brenhinedd, from Brutus to Cadwaladr. Then came Brut y Tywysogion, which begins with the death of Cadwaladr in 682 and runs on to the fall of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd in 1282. Read together, they told the history of one people, from the walls of Troy to the last prince of Wales.",
   "One version, known as Brut Tysilio after Saint Tysilio of Meifod, was printed in 1801 in the Myvyrian Archaiology of Wales by Owain Myfyr, Iolo Morganwg and William Owen Pughe, and in 1811 Peter Roberts put it into English as The Chronicle of the Kings of Britain.",
   "## The three names of the island",
   "The Welsh Triads, Trioedd Ynys Prydain, kept their own memory of the island’s first days. They give it three names. Before it was settled it was Clas Myrddin, Myrddin’s Precinct. After it was settled it was Y Fêl Ynys, the Honey Isle. And when Prydain son of Aedd Mawr won it, it was called Ynys Prydain, the Island of Britain. The old learning of the triads and the story of Brutus stood side by side in Welsh books, and both said the same thing: that this island was the inheritance of the Britons, and the Cymry were their heirs.",
   "## Gerald of Wales",
   "When Gerald of Wales wrote his Description of Wales in 1194, he began with Brutus. Cambria, he wrote, was named after Camber, son of Brutus, who received the land beyond the Severn when the realm was shared between the brothers. And Gerald tells of the old man of Pencader, who told King Henry II in 1163 that no nation but this of Wales, and no language but theirs, would answer on the day of judgement for this corner of the earth. It was the faith of a people who knew that the island had been theirs from the beginning.",
   "## Owain Glyndŵr writes to Scotland",
   "When Owain Glyndŵr rose for the freedom of Wales, he wrote in 1401 to Robert III, King of Scots, and he began with Brutus:",
   "> Brutus, your most noble ancestor and mine, was the first crowned king who dwelt in this realm of England, which of old times was called Great Britain. The which Brutus begat three sons, to wit, Albanact, Locrine, and Camber; from which same Albanact you are descended in direct line. | Owain Glyndŵr to Robert III, 1401, from the chronicle of Adam of Usk, translated by E. M. Thompson (1904)",
   "The line of Camber, Owain went on, reigned royally down to Cadwaladr, the last crowned king of the Britons, from whom he himself descended in direct line. He wrote as one prince of the blood of Brutus to another, cousin to cousin, and asked for help against their common enemy.",
   "## The son of prophecy",
   "Geoffrey’s History ended with a promise. When Cadwaladr went to Rome, an angel’s voice told him that the Britons would not rule the whole island again until the time Myrddin had foretold, and that one day, through the merit of their faith, they would win it back. For three hundred years the Welsh poets sang of the Mab Darogan, the son of prophecy, who would restore the crown of Britain to the line of Brutus and Cadwaladr.",
   "In 1485 the Welsh believed the prophecy had come true. Harri Tudur, born at Pembroke, landed at Dale, marched through Wales under the red dragon of Cadwaladr, and won the crown at Bosworth. Soon afterwards he sent learned men into Wales to set down his descent. Gutun Owain, poet and herald, of Basingwerk, was one of their chief authorities, and the roll they made traced the new king’s line back through the princes of Wales and Cadwaladr to Brutus himself. Henry put the red dragon into the royal arms, and named his first son Arthur.",
   "## Wales defends the British history",
   "For the Tudors the story of Brutus was the history of their own family, and Welsh scholars rose to defend it. When the Italian Polydore Vergil cast doubt on it in his history of England, first printed in 1534, Sir John Price of Brecon, the man who printed the first book in Welsh, answered him with his Defence of the British History, printed in 1573. Humphrey Llwyd of Denbigh stood up for the history of the Britons in his writings, and David Powel’s Historie of Cambria of 1584 carried the story of Wales into the age of print.",
   "When James VI of Scotland became king of England in 1603, the poets hailed him as a second Brutus. He was the great-grandson of Margaret Tudor, daughter of Harri Tudur, and in him the three lands that Brutus had shared among his sons were joined again under one crown. He took the title King of Great Britain. In the London pageant of 1605, The Triumphs of Re-united Britannia, Locrinus, Camber and Albanactus came on stage and laid down their crowns before him. Through the blood of a Welshman, the island of Brutus was one again.",
   "## In the poets",
   "The story ran through the poetry of the whole island. Edmund Spenser set out the line of British kings from Brutus in his Faerie Queene, Michael Drayton told of Brutus in his Poly-Olbion, and Shakespeare took King Lear and Cymbeline from Geoffrey’s pages. All of it grew from the memory of the Britons of Wales.",
   "In Wales the story lived on in the people’s own books. Theophilus Evans’s Drych y Prif Oesoedd, the Mirror of the First Ages, first printed in 1716, told the Welsh the glories of their beginnings, from Gomer son of Japheth to Brutus and the kings of the Britons. It was printed again and again and read in farmhouses across Wales until Victorian times.",
   "## What the story meant to Wales",
   "To the Welsh, the story of Brutus was their title deed. It said that the Cymry were not latecomers or a remnant, but the first and rightful people of the whole Island of Britain: kin to the Romans through Aeneas, heirs of the oldest crown in the island, and speakers of the language Brutus brought from Troy. It gave Wales a history as long and as noble as any nation in Europe, and the hope that the island would one day be theirs again.",
   "Historians argue over how old the story is and where it came from. What is beyond argument is what it meant. Princes wrote it in their letters, poets sang it, monks copied it into their chronicles for four hundred years, and a Welsh king carried it to the throne. Welsh tradition holds it as the beginning of the story of the Britons, and for a thousand years the Cymry carried it with pride."],
